The overall reaction is:
X + Y^{2+} \rightarrow Y + X^{2+}
Standard cell potential \(E^{0}_{Cell}\) is calculated as:
E^{0}_{Cell} = E^{0}_{Cathode} - E^{0}_{Anode}
Here, Y is reduced (cathode) and X is oxidized (anode).
E^{0}_{Cell} = 0.36 - (-2.36) = 2.72 \text{ V}
Using the Nernst equation for the cell potential \(E_{Cell}\):
E_{Cell} = E^{0}_{Cell} - \frac{2.303RT}{nF} \log Q
Given \(\frac{2.303RT}{F} = 0.06\) V and \(n=2\) (since \(X \rightarrow X^{2+}\) and \(Y^{2+} \rightarrow Y\)).
The reaction quotient \(Q\) is \(Q = \frac{[X^{2+}]}{[Y^{2+}]}\).
E_{Cell} = 2.72 - \frac{0.06}{2} \log \frac{[X^{2+}]}{[Y^{2+}]}
Substitute the given concentrations:
E_{Cell} = 2.72 - \frac{0.06}{2} \log \frac{0.001}{0.01}
E_{Cell} = 2.72 - 0.03 \log (0.1)
E_{Cell} = 2.72 - 0.03 (-1)
E_{Cell} = 2.72 + 0.03 = 2.75 \text{ V}
To express the answer in the format \(\times 10^{-2}\) V:
E_{Cell} = 2.75 \text{ V} = 275 \times 10^{-2} \text{ V}
The nearest integer is 275.

